{
  "version": 267,
  "released": "2026-08-30",
  "changelog": "v267: EVENT IDENTITY, PHASE I1 — THE FOLD APPLIES BINDINGS. I0 settled what the fold WOULD decide, as pure functions with fixtures, because the auditor's condition for starting work was that authority, identity, migration and recovery semantics be decided before the phases that consume them. This is the phase that consumes them: `derive_calculated_timeline` gains one input and the identity layer starts changing drawings. The controlling design is lifehug-platform `docs/design/event-identity.md` v4 (§3.5, §5.1–§5.6, and §5.8's lifecycle matrix rows 1, 3 and 10 proved at the fold level); this repo's summary is ADR 0031. There is still NO BINDER — nothing here decides that two tellings are one thing. Records arrive already made, I2 is what makes them, and `compile` stays zero-model.\n\nEVERY FINGERPRINT MOVES. `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` is `timeline-rules:5`. Grouping is the first input every other phase reads, so every node's `input_fingerprint` moves with this bump whether or not that node is bound — not a side effect, the honest signal: a projection calculated by `:4` rules is stale everywhere, not only where a binding landed. A host pinning the version bumps it; a published projection re-derives. `PROJECTION_SCHEMA_VERSION` does NOT move — every new field is additive, absent means unchanged, and a v2 reader reads a v2 payload.\n\nWHY, IN ONE VAULT. Executed on the founder's own at `5690d37e`: 881 nodes, 703 unplaced, and Etherfuse alone is six of them — a dated work-history landmark, a dated conversation about the idea, and four UNDATED classifier occurrences of the same company. Needs-placing is inflated with facts the person already dated somewhere else, and Play can ask \"when did you start Etherfuse?\" after he said May 2022. The fixture in this release is that shape with synthetic words: five tellings fold into ONE node with one reconciled date, seven \"when did this happen?\" questions become three, and the four the bind answered are gone.\n\nWHAT THE FOLD NOW READS. `episode_records` — operations, bindings and an optional telling manifest — is a SIBLING of `event_resolution_records` and never touches `event_ref`, whose v247 era meaning is untouched: an era-bound claim is refused as a binding target and reported at fold time, and a telling about an event that happened WITHIN an era keeps full episode eligibility. `episode_fold.load_episode_records` is the one impure function; it reads BOTH authorities, because a grouping authority that ignored half its records would draw a timeline nobody filed.\n\nNOTHING HERE RE-IMPLEMENTS AN I0 DECISION, and that is the point of having had an I0. `grouping_key`, `entailed_not_same`, `possible_outer_range`, `node_aliases`, `episode_aliases` and `fold_diagnostics` are CALLED; `event_identity`'s loaders, validators and envelope reader are CALLED. `system/episode_fold.py` is wiring, the enrichment a binding needs to reach the frozen minter, and per-node assembly. It holds no rule of its own.\n\nPUBLISHED, ADDITIVELY. Per node: `episode_id`, `tellings`, `telling_count`, `identity_origins`, `containments`, `related`, `proposed_links`. The first four ride an EPISODE node only, because publishing `telling_count: 1` on every node in a vault that has never bound anything would be a schema change dressed as a fact. Per envelope: `node_aliases`, `episode_aliases`, `identity_rule_version`, `identity_diagnostics`. The first three are SERVED by `calculated_view` — a host that cannot see them cannot resolve a node id a bind superseded, which is O-E1b's own finding applied to a new layer — and `identity_diagnostics` is excused by name, for `diagnostics`' reason: it is a maintainer's surface, and a person's page showing it would be the system talking about itself.\n\nENTAILMENT IS COMPUTED, NEVER STORED. `same(A,E) ∧ not_same(B,E) ⇒ not_same(A,B)` is published as a view of this generation, and retracting either premise removes the pair in the same breath. Storing the closure is how a retracted binding leaves a permanent phantom negative behind, and the only way to guarantee it disappears is never to have persisted it.\n\nCONTAINMENT INHERITS A POSSIBILITY, NOT A DATE. A member the person put INSIDE an episode and never dated renders the episode's own span in `possible_temporal_value`, beside E3's `within` and never in `best_temporal_value`. Every clause of §5.3 is structural rather than checked afterwards: the bounds are the episode's own, copied; the rule reads `member_value is None` and nothing else; `anchors` is empty; nothing is written; and `placed` is untouched, so the member's precision question is minted exactly as it was before the containment existed. Two containing episodes placed differently is NO pick at all — an ambiguity is a question for I3, never a guess made in a fold.\n\nTHE REFUSALS ARE LOUD AND EARLY, raised while the adapter is constructed and before a node is drawn, because a fold that drew half a projection and then refused would be worse than one that refused. `identity_conflict` on the NARROW reading I0 pinned — two `same` bindings, or one pair decided twice, and NOT a telling that carries `same` to one episode and `not_same` to every episode already rejected, which is the five-answer model the product needs. `identity_envelope_incomplete` from the loader, so an operation naming a binding the vault does not hold is never a partially applied episode. A dormant binding is reported and ignored, because a retracted claim is not a bug in the binding.\n\nC1'S NAMED GAP IS CLOSED. `classifier_claims.document_revision()` declares the STORY's own `content_sha256` — the thing a person corrects — as distinct from the classification's revision, which moves whenever the model rewords a title; the two were indistinguishable, so the manifest re-keyed nothing and said so. Now a rewording at an unchanged story reaches the EVIDENCE rung and a real source correction says `telling_source_corrected`. `landmark_projection` declares its telling key, the promoted record's revision and its entry id. Where a revision is genuinely undeclarable — a source that cannot be read, or whose bytes have drifted under the claims citing it — the conservative reading is unchanged and still says so by name.\n\nONE CARRIED FIELD ON `adopt`. The create envelope lives under `state/`, deleting it is a supported act, and an adopted episode whose node id could not be re-derived from `sources/` alone is exactly the orphan G1 refuses — so an adopt now CARRIES `canonical_event_kind` the way it already carries the creation's canonical inputs. It is outside `OPERATION_IDENTITY_KEYS`, so no digest moves.\n\nONE PLACE THE DESIGN COULD NOT BE HONORED AS WRITTEN, named rather than papered over. §3.5 mints an episode's node id from `derive_node_id(node_kind=\"episode\", canonical_event_kind, subject_keys, discriminator=episode_id)`. The frozen minter, the unchanged keys and the episode id as the discriminator are exactly that; `subject_keys` is EMPTY (`episode_fold.EPISODE_SUBJECT_KEYS`), and the reason is Law 5. The episode id is already a complete discriminator, so any content-derived component would add nothing to uniqueness while moving the node id every time a member joined, a subject resolved or a claim was retracted — and an episode whose node id churns takes an open session, a Mirror row and a URL with it. Named in one place so I2 or the founder review can change the decision once.\n\nPROVEN TO FIRE. Eighteen deliberate one-line mutations were each applied to a built tree and the suite SEEN going red: grouping never applies; a containment overrides a stated value; a containment picks the first of two; the loader stops reading envelopes; an unknown input key is accepted silently; the manifest view degrades to a bare index (the real bug this caught — every binding read as dormant while the projection stayed correct, which is the worst way for a diagnostic to be wrong); `adopt` stops carrying the canonical kind; `node_aliases` are not published; entailment is not computed; an episode block on a non-episode node is accepted; `telling_count` is believed; a link naming no episode is accepted; links are left in caller order (twice, in both modules that sort them); the classifier declares no document revision; the recorder declares no tellings; the episode's node id forgets its episode; proposals are allowed to group. All eighteen turned the suite red. The two link-order mutations needed a fixture whose binding DIGESTS sort the opposite way to the link key, found by search rather than chosen — a guard against an ordering can only fire where the two orderings actually disagree.\n\nCERT-11, THE OSS HALF. Deleting `state/temporal_claims/identities/` and the telling manifest, then folding the same claims with no sources-side records applied, reproduces the pre-binding projection byte-identically. A second row proves the sources-side records ALONE rebuild the same partition and the same node id after every byte under `state/` is gone. A third permutes claims against bindings four ways and compares one structural signature. The three frozen key lists — `CLAIM_IDENTITY_KEYS`, `NODE_IDENTITY_KEYS`, `WORK_ITEM_IDENTITY_KEYS` — are byte-identical to v264, and a binding record never modifies a claim or a telling: their bytes and ids are identical before and after.",
